"2082-2082:U\",\n    \"2083-2093:A\",\n    \"2094-2095:U\",\n    \"2096-2103:B\",\n    \"2104-2105:O\",\n    \"2106-2107:A\",\n    \"2108-2108:U\",\n    \"2109-2137:A\",\n    \"2138-2139:U\",\n    \"2140-2146:A\",\n    \"2147-2147:U\",\n    \"2148-2152:B\",\n    \"2153-2157:A\",\n    \"2158-2164:B\",\n    \"2165-2166:A\",\n    \"2167-2167:U\",\n    \"2168-2171:B\",\n    \"2172-2174:A\",\n    \"2175-2189:B\",\n    \"2190-2197:A\",\n    \"2198-2202:B\",\n    \"2203-2203:U\",\n    \"2204-2208:B\",\n    \"2209-2209:A\",\n    \"2210-2210:U\",\n    \"2211-2216:B\",\n    \"2217-2217:U\",\n    \"2218-2219:A\",\n    \"2220-2229:B\",\n    \"2230-2230:U\",\n    \"2231-2235:A\",\n    \"2236-2244:B\",\n    \"2245-2245:U\",\n    \"2246-2248:A\",\n    \"2249-2249:U\",\n    \"2250-2257:O\",\n    \"2258-2258:U\",\n    \"2259-2264:B\",\n    \"2265-2265:U\",\n    \"2266-2267:A\",\n    \"2268-2268:U\",\n    \"2269-2277:B\",\n    \"2278-2279:U\",\n    \"2280-2283:A\",\n    \"2284-2284:U\",\n    \"2285-2285:B\",\n    \"2286-2286:U\",\n    \"2287-2289:A\",\n    \"2290-2293:O\",\n    \"2294-2312:O\",\n    \"2313-2314:U\",\n    \"2315-2316:A\",\n    \"2317-2317:B\",\n    \"2318-2318:A\",\n    \"2319-2319:U\",\n    \"2320-2329:A\",\n    \"2330-2330:U\",\n    \"2331-2332:A\",\n    \"2333-2333:B\",\n    \"2334-2337:U\",\n    \"2338-2349:A\",\n    \"2350-2350:U\",\n    \"2351-2353:B\",\n    \"2354-2360:A\",\n    \"2361-2361:U\",\n    \"2362-2394:A\",\n    \"2395-2395:U\",\n    \"2396-2402:A\",\n    \"2403-2403:U\",\n    \"2404-2404:O\",\n    \"2405-2418:B\",\n    \"2419-2430:A\",\n    \"2431-2431:U\",\n    \"2432-2432:O\",\n    \"2433-2433:U\",\n    \"2434-2453:B\",\n    \"2454-2454:U\",\n    \"2455-2492:A\",\n    \"2493-2512:O\"\n  ],\n  \"teaserSpans\": [\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 0,\n      \"endEvent\": 18,\n      \"reason\": \"Opening edited montage replays later exchanges: the raised Jesus conclusion at E1731–1732, journal challenge at E1750–1753, expectation challenge at E719–721, interruptions, and arm-wrestling remark at E1963–1964.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"candidates\": [\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1181,\n      \"endEvent\": 1202,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1174,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1249,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"I don't think God can break the law of physics anymore than he can break the law of mathematics\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"none\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The categorical analogy could exclude resurrection evidence before assessment, but this is an ordinary argument answering a question about personal disbelief. A subsequently permits extraordinary evidence and evidence from physicists (E1223–1239), and grants a hypothetical violation at E1431–1436. Those qualifications prevent confirming an exceptionless protective rule.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1354,\n      \"endEvent\": 1357,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 1320,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 1387,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"Jesus is completely unparalleled, completely different than all these rest\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"unclear\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"identity\", \"ridicule\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The affirmation dismisses proposed religious parallels as insane, potentially substituting uniqueness for an explanation of their evidential irrelevance. However, B has just specified an enemy-conversion distinction (E1341–1352) and previously offered witness and continuity arguments. The sweeping dismissal overreaches those reasons, but does not clearly make every comparison inadmissible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 2198,\n      \"endEvent\": 2227,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 2175,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 2248,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"maybe it's because that person has more open heart and they're responding in some way\",\n      \"form\": \"no\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"shame\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Responding to selective appearances, B invokes God's wish for humility and recipients' unseen openness, potentially insulating the appearance argument from nonappearance. Yet he expressly says he cannot specify why (E2220–2222) and disclaims certainty (E2236–2238). This tentative theological explanation does not clearly neutralize every contrary pattern or condemn every nonbeliever.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 2472,\n      \"endEvent\": 2485,\n      \"contextStartEvent\": 2455,\n      \"contextEndEvent\": 2492,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"You can't have religious arguments. You have to have historical arguments.\",\n      \"form\": \"yes\",\n      \"substitution\": \"no\",\n      \"protection\": \"unclear\",\n      \"emotionalDevices\": [\"shame\"],\n      \"confidence\": \"medium\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A draws a categorical disciplinary boundary and associates crossing it with misleading listeners. This could exclude a rival argument by classification, but he supplies criteria: critical source evaluation and probability (E2486–2490), developed earlier through comparisons. The text does not establish that relevant evidence is excluded regardless of its merits.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"contrasts\": [\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1021,\n      \"endEvent\": 1047,\n      \"speaker\": \"A\",\n      \"quote\": \"Paul, but he doesn't actually say what he saw.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A explains the missing descriptive evidence, lists possible experiences, and expressly declines to identify which occurred. The conclusion is limited by the sources rather than supplied by a catchphrase.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"startEvent\": 1738,\n      \"endEvent\": 1744,\n      \"speaker\": \"B\",\n      \"quote\": \"The Christian movement didn't have to survive.\",\n      \"explanation\": \"B identifies possible nonfulfillment of the worldwide-spread claim, supplying a reason for his preceding conclusion. At E1827–1837 he also applies the proposed reasoning hypothetically to Mormonism. The disputed inference remains an articulated argument, not an unsupported slogan.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}}
